---
import BaseLayout from '~/components/BaseLayout.astro';
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro';
import CodeBlock from '~/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import { VERSIONS } from '~/data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Yeptris — validation and schema docs"
  description="Four distinct faces: parse-time schema modes, the schema-family validation walk (core and the JSON productions), JSON Schema draft 2020-12, and schema descriptors that materialize typed columns in one C pass. C, Ruby, and Python, every snippet executed against the shipped artifacts."
>
  <DocsShell
    crumb="Yeptris — validation and schemas"
    title={`Yeptris — validation and schemas (engine v${VERSIONS.yaml})`}
    lede="Yeptris ships four distinct faces that are easy to blur: the parse-time schema MODES (YAML 1.2 core vs 1.1-compat — how implicit typing resolves), the schema-family VALIDATION walk (spec-conformance diagnostics, core and the spec 10.2 JSON productions), JSON SCHEMA draft 2020-12 (the keyword subset, schema bytes parsed by the same engine), and schema DESCRIPTORS (typed columns materialized in one C pass — no intermediate document). This page keeps them apart and documents each with snippets executed against the shipped artifacts."
  >
    <Callout kind="note">
      Every snippet on this page was executed against the released
      yeptris 0.6.63 artifacts before publishing — the Ruby snippets
      against the 0.6.63.0 gem, the C snippets compiled against the
      v0.6.63 engine (<code>&lt;yeptris/validate.h&gt;</code>), the
      Python snippets against the 0.6.63.0 package. Python carries the
      validation faces (the family walk, JSON Schema, the compiled
      schema); the descriptor materialization stays Ruby-only.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="faces">The four faces, kept apart</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>1. Parse-time schema modes</strong> —
      <code>Document.parse(src, schema: :core_12)</code> vs
      <code>:compat_11</code>: which implicit productions resolve during
      the parse (1.2 core vs the 1.1-compat family psych rides). This is
      a parse input, not a validation verdict.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>2. The schema-family validation walk</strong> —
      <code>document.validate(schema:)</code> /
      <code>yeptris_validate</code>: the document is parsed, then checked
      against the YAML 1.2 spec's own conformance rules — explicit-tag
      consistency (spec 10.3 resolution), canonical-form mapping-key
      uniqueness (spec 3.2.1.3 — <code>0o13</code> and <code>0xB</code>
      are the same key). Under <code>:json_12</code> the same walk swaps
      in the spec 10.2 JSON productions: implicit resolution stays
      JSON-shaped (the core extensions — <code>0o</code>/<code>0x</code>
      radix, <code>+12.3</code>, the <code>True</code>/<code>~</code>
      casings — stay strings per example 10.8), mapping keys must be
      strings, and explicit non-JSON tags are rejected. Whole reports,
      not callbacks.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>3. JSON Schema validation</strong> —
      <code>yeptris_json_schema_validate</code>: the schema arrives as
      bytes and is parsed by the same engine (JSON is YAML flow), the
      target is validated against the draft 2020-12 keyword subset;
      unknown keywords are ignored, per 2020-12. Compile the schema once
      and validate many instance streams against the retained model with
      <code>yeptris_schema_compile</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>4. Schema descriptors</strong> —
      <code>Yeptris::Schema</code>: one native pass fills typed columns
      against a caller-compiled descriptor (KIND scalar/sequence/mapping,
      TYPE str/int/float/bool/null/any, REQUIRED, FIRST_WINS duplicate
      policy) — the intermediate generic document never exists. This is
      the lutaml-model KV path (issue #184).
    </p>

    <h2 id="walk">The validation walk (Ruby)</h2>
    <p>
      <code>Document.parse</code> under a schema, then
      <code>validate</code> — an array of
      <code>Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic</code>; empty means valid.
      A duplicate key (the diagnostic carries path, 1-based line/col,
      a numeric code, and a bounded detail):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "yeptris"

doc = Yeptris::Document.parse("---\\nname: x\\nname: y\\n", schema: :core_12)
doc.validate(schema: :core_12)
# => [#<Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic duplicate-key path="" line=3 col=1
#      detail="duplicate mapping key (\\"name\\")">]`}
    />
    <p>
      Canonical-form key uniqueness: <code>0o13</code> and
      <code>0xB</code> both resolve to 11, so as keys of one mapping
      they collide:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`doc = Yeptris::Document.parse("---\\n0o13: x\\n0xB: y\\n", schema: :core_12)
doc.validate(schema: :core_12)
# => [#<Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic duplicate-key path="" line=3 col=1
#      detail="duplicate mapping key (\\"0xB\\")">]`}
    />

    <h2 id="json">The JSON productions family (:json_12)</h2>
    <p>
      <code>schema: :json_12</code> applies the spec 10.2 restriction:
      mapping keys must be strings (a complex key is a violation), and
      an explicit non-JSON tag is rejected with the instance path:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`doc = Yeptris::Document.parse("? [complex, key]\\n: v\\n", schema: :json_12)
doc.validate(schema: :json_12)
# => [#<Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic key-not-string path="" line=1 col=4
#      detail="mapping key is a sequence">]

doc = Yeptris::Document.parse("---\\na: !!custom v\\n", schema: :json_12)
doc.validate(schema: :json_12)
# => [#<Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic non-json-type path="/a" line=2 col=13
#      detail="explicit custom tag is not a JSON type">]`}
    />
    <p>
      The core-only productions stay strings here —
      <code>a: True</code> under <code>:json_12</code> is a plain string
      <code>"True"</code> (spec example 10.8), so it validates clean:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`doc = Yeptris::Document.parse("---\\na: True\\n", schema: :json_12)
doc.validate(schema: :json_12)
# => []`}
    />

    <h2 id="jsonschema">JSON Schema validation (draft 2020-12)</h2>
    <p>
      <code>yeptris_json_schema_validate</code> takes the schema and the
      target as bytes — the schema is parsed by the same engine (JSON is
      YAML flow; document 0 of the schema stream is the schema), then
      every document of the target stream is validated against it. The
      keyword subset is draft 2020-12: <code>type</code>,
      <code>enum</code>, <code>const</code>, <code>pattern</code>; the
      numeric <code>minimum</code>/<code>maximum</code>/exclusive bounds
      and <code>multipleOf</code>; <code>minLength</code>/
      <code>maxLength</code> (code points); <code>items</code>/
      <code>prefixItems</code>, <code>minItems</code>/
      <code>maxItems</code>, <code>uniqueItems</code>,
      <code>contains</code> (+
      <code>minContains</code>/<code>maxContains</code>);
      <code>properties</code>, <code>required</code>,
      <code>patternProperties</code>, <code>propertyNames</code>,
      <code>minProperties</code>/<code>maxProperties</code>,
      <code>additionalProperties</code>;
      <code>dependentRequired</code>/<code>dependentSchemas</code>;
      <code>if</code>/<code>then</code>/<code>else</code>;
      <code>allOf</code>/<code>anyOf</code>/<code>oneOf</code>/
      <code>not</code>; local <code>$ref</code> (<code>$defs</code> and
      <code>definitions</code> both resolve); boolean schemas. Unknown
      keywords — and known keywords with unreadable values — are
      ignored, per 2020-12. Instance types ride the engine's core-schema
      resolution (the YAML→JSON data model): <code>"y"</code> is
      boolean, <code>1.0</code> is integer.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <yeptris/validate.h>

int main(void) {
    const char *schema =
        "{ \\"type\\": \\"object\\","
        "  \\"properties\\": { \\"id\\": { \\"type\\": \\"integer\\" } },"
        "  \\"required\\": [\\"id\\"] }";
    const char *doc = "---\\nid: \\"42\\"\\n";

    yeptris_validate_report *report = NULL;
    YeptrisStatus st = yeptris_json_schema_validate(schema, strlen(schema),
                                                    doc, strlen(doc), &report);
    if (st != YEPTRIS_OK && st != YEPTRIS_ERROR_SCHEMA) return 1;

    size_t n = yeptris_validate_report_count(report);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        const yeptris_diagnostic *d = yeptris_validate_report_at(report, i);
        printf("code=%u path=%s line=%u col=%u detail=%s\\n",
               d->code, d->path, d->line, d->col, d->detail);
    }
    yeptris_validate_report_free(report);
    return 0;
}
/* code=5 path=/id line=2 col=6 detail=expected integer, got string */`}
    />
    <p>
      The COMPILED SCHEMA — parse once, validate many
      (<code>yeptris_schema_compile</code> retains the schema bytes, its
      DOM, and the pattern kernel's compiled programs;
      <code>yeptris_schema_validate</code> keeps the exact status/report
      contract; <code>yeptris_schema_free</code> releases everything in
      one call). The handle is immutable after compile: one handle per
      thread like documents, read-only sharing safe:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <yeptris/validate.h>

int main(void) {
    const char *schema = "{ \\"type\\": \\"integer\\" }";
    yeptris_schema *compiled = yeptris_schema_compile(schema, strlen(schema));
    if (!compiled) return 1;

    const char *streams[] = { "12\\n", "---\\ntrue\\n" };
    for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        yeptris_validate_report *report = NULL;
        yeptris_schema_validate(compiled, streams[i], strlen(streams[i]), &report);
        size_t n = yeptris_validate_report_count(report);
        for (size_t j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            const yeptris_diagnostic *d = yeptris_validate_report_at(report, j);
            printf("stream %d: code=%u path=%s line=%u col=%u detail=%s\\n",
                   i, d->code, d->path, d->line, d->col, d->detail);
        }
        yeptris_validate_report_free(report);
    }
    yeptris_schema_free(compiled);
    return 0;
}
/* stream 1: code=5 path= line=2 col=1 detail=expected integer, got boolean */`}
    />
    <p>
      The same faces in Ruby — <code>Yeptris::YAML.validate</code> with
      <code>json_schema:</code> (bytes, one-shot) or a compiled
      <code>Yeptris::Validation::Schema</code>; <code>load</code> takes
      the same keyword and raises
      <code>Yeptris::Validation::Error</code> carrying the diagnostics
      before loading a byte:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "yeptris"

schema = '{ "type": "object",
  "properties": { "id": { "type": "integer" } },
  "required": ["id"] }'

Yeptris::YAML.validate("---\\nid: 42\\n", json_schema: schema)
# => []
Yeptris::YAML.validate("---\\nid: \\"42\\"\\n", json_schema: schema)
# => [#<Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic type path="/id" line=2 col=6
#      detail="expected integer, got string">]

Yeptris::YAML.load("---\\nid: \\"42\\"\\n", json_schema: schema)
# => Yeptris::Validation::Error: validation failed (1 diagnostics):
#      type at /id: expected integer, got string`}
    />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`Yeptris::Validation::Schema.open(schema) do |compiled|
  compiled.valid?("id: 7\\n") # => true
  compiled.validate("---\\nname: n\\n")
  # => [#<Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic required path="/id" line=2 col=1
  #      detail="missing required property \\"id\\"">]
end`}
    />
    <p>
      And in Python — <code>yeptris.yaml</code> carries
      <code>validate</code>/<code>is_valid</code> (one-shot bytes or a
      compiled schema), the <code>safe_load</code> gate
      (<code>json_schema=</code> validates the whole stream before any
      document is built, raising <code>yeptris.ValidationError</code>),
      and <code>compile_schema</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="python"
      code={`import yeptris.yaml as y

schema = '{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "integer" } }, "required": ["id"] }'

y.validate('---\\nid: "42"\\n', json_schema=schema)
# => [Diagnostic(code=5, keyword='type', path=['id'],
#                 message='expected integer, got string', line=2, col=6)]
y.is_valid('---\\nid: 42\\n', json_schema=schema)  # => True
y.safe_load('---\\nid: 42\\n', json_schema=schema) # => {'id': 42}

compiled = y.compile_schema(schema)
compiled.is_valid("id: 7\\n") # => True
compiled.validate("name: n\\n")
# => [Diagnostic(code=6, keyword='required', path=['id'],
#                 message='missing required property "id"', line=1, col=1)]`}
    />

    <h2 id="diagnostics">Diagnostic anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      Every diagnostic — C, Ruby, and Python — carries the same shape:
      <code>path</code> (a JSON-pointer instance path, <code>/a/b/0</code>
      for array items, <code>~0</code>/<code>~1</code> escaped, root
      relative per document; multi-document streams prefix the document
      index — <code>/1/a/b</code> is <code>a/b</code> of document 1),
      <code>line</code>/<code>col</code> (1-based; 0 = unknown — arena-
      owned scalars have no input position, the path still names them),
      a stable numeric <code>code</code> (the <code>YEPTRIS_DIAG_*</code>
      enum in <code>&lt;yeptris/validate.h&gt;</code> — appending only;
      the bindings hard-code the values, the ABI test pins the head, and
      each binding spells the code as a keyword:
      <code>duplicate-key</code>/<code>type</code> in Ruby,
      <code>duplicateKey</code>/<code>type</code> in Python), and a
      bounded <code>detail</code> string. The document-index prefix is
      what names a miss in a stream — a bad second document reports its
      own path, not the first document's:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`schema = '{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "integer" } } }'
Yeptris::YAML.validate("---\\nid: 1\\n---\\nid: \\"x\\"\\n", json_schema: schema)
# => [#<Yeptris::Validation::Diagnostic type path="/1/id" line=4 col=6
#      detail="expected integer, got string">]`}
    />

    <h2 id="c">C</h2>
    <p>
      <code>yeptris_validate</code> parses and validates in one call —
      every document of the stream. An already-parsed document or stream
      validates without re-parsing:
      <code>yeptris_validate_document</code> (the document is not
      modified; the report borrows nothing from it). Statuses compose:
      <code>YEPTRIS_OK</code> (valid — empty report) or
      <code>YEPTRIS_ERROR_SCHEMA</code> (the report carries diagnostics).
      Every diagnostic is a flat <code>yeptris_diagnostic</code> struct
      (16 + 256 + 128 bytes, ABI-pinned); the report frees in one call:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <yeptris/validate.h>

int main(void) {
    const char *doc = "---\\nname: x\\nname: y\\n";
    yeptris_validate_report *report = NULL;
    YeptrisStatus st = yeptris_validate(doc, strlen(doc), YEPTRIS_SCHEMA_12_CORE, &report);
    if (st != YEPTRIS_OK && st != YEPTRIS_ERROR_SCHEMA) return 1;

    size_t n = yeptris_validate_report_count(report);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        const yeptris_diagnostic *d = yeptris_validate_report_at(report, i);
        printf("code=%u path=%s line=%u col=%u detail=%s\\n",
               d->code, d->path, d->line, d->col, d->detail);
    }
    yeptris_validate_report_free(report);
    return 0;
}
/* code=4 path= line=3 col=1 detail=duplicate mapping key ("name") */`}
    />

    <h2 id="descriptors">Schema descriptors</h2>
    <p>
      The descriptor face compiles a flat table (the root entry first;
      container entries point at their children via
      <code>child_index</code>/<code>child_count</code>) and one native
      pass fills typed columns — lutaml-model's KV path rides this.
      Engine tags after 0.6.12 carry the executed
      <code>:any</code> verdict —
      <code>Yeptris::Schema.headroom_supported?</code> probes the loaded
      engine rather than trusting a version:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "yeptris"

desc = [
  { kind: :mapping, child_index: 1, child_count: 3 },
  { wire_name: "id",    kind: :scalar, type: :int },
  { wire_name: "name",  kind: :scalar, type: :str },
  { wire_name: "active", kind: :scalar, type: :bool },
]
Yeptris::Schema.load_records("id: 42\\nname: widget\\nactive: true\\n", desc: desc)
# => [{ id: 42, name: "widget", active: true }]`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>load_records</code> zips the columns into record hashes:
      mapping root → one hash, sequence-of-mappings → one hash per
      element, sequence-of-scalars → <code>{'{ value: ... }'}</code> hashes.
      Absent keys are <em>omitted</em> (not nil-filled) — absent seeds
      defaults; explicit nil is a present value. The
      <code>:any</code> type rides the C tag after engine 0.6.12 —
      <code>headroom_supported?</code> gates it.
    </p>

    <Callout kind="note">
      <strong>Python</strong>: the yeptris package carries the
      validation faces — <code>yeptris.yaml.validate</code>/
      <code>is_valid</code> (the family walk and JSON Schema),
      <code>safe_load(..., json_schema=/validate=)</code>,
      <code>compile_schema</code>. What it does not carry is the
      schema-descriptor materialization — <code>Yeptris::Schema</code>
      stays Ruby-only.
    </Callout>
  </DocsShell>
</BaseLayout>
